<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vale of Clwyd</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vale of Clwyd is the broad valley of the River Clwyd in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, running from the hills south of Ruthin to the sea at Rhyl. Fertile and sheltered, it is lined by the Clwydian Range with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillforts to the east. The historic towns of Ruthin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbigh">Denbigh</a> and the cathedral city of St Asaph lie within it.</p></div>
